WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.830
If you open a Bible to Matthew 2:16-18, you’ll
encounter one of the most notorious stories

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.070
in any religion.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:12.520
Suspicious of a prophesy about a new king,
Herod the Great tricks the Magi into telling

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.330
him where baby Jesus was born, then sends
his soldiers to massacre every child under

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.410
the age of two in Bethlehem.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:25.980
It’s a scene of shocking cruelty, one that
has ensured King Herod’s name is still synonymous

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:34.730
with evil today.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.330
But what if we told you there was more to
Herod’s story?

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:44.140
Born in southern Palestine in 73 BC, Herod
was a man at the center of events that shaped

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.140
history.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:49.260
He was there when Rome transformed from a
republic to an empire.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:55.610
He consorted with Julius Caesar, befriended
Mark Antony, and submitted to the rule of

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:56.610
Augustus.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.330
As king of Judea, he rebuilt the Second Temple,
saved the Olympic Games from bankruptcy, and

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.950
projected Jewish power across the world.

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:10.450
Yet he died an unloved tyrant, hated by his
people, his name forever stained with the

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:11.970
blood of the innocent.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:19.340
This is the story of how one great man became
the tyrant king of history.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:27.890
The Last Days of Judea
When Herod was born in 73 BC, it was a great

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.560
time to be from a Jewish family.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.950
Roughly a hundred years before, a group of
priests known as the Maccabees had rebelled

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.650
against the Seleucid Empire and turned Jerusalem
into an independent city state.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.990
For the first time in a long time, the Jews
were beholden to no-one.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:48.359
That “no-one” included the superpower
of the era, the mighty Roman Republic, with

00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:51.020
whose blessing Judea kept its independence.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:57.079
Unfortunately for Herod, his family’s Jewish
roots weren’t as clear cut as he might have

00:01:57.079 --> 00:01:58.079
liked.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:03.380
The son of a wealthy, ambitious man known
as Antipater, Herod was by birth part Edomite,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.380
part Arab.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.670
Although his father’s family had been practicing
Jews for a century, they were still seen as

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:13.730
outsiders in Judea, a stigma that kept the
family from power.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:17.210
But Herod’s pop Antipater was nothing if
not ambitious.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:23.810
And there was no way he was going to let his
murky origins halt his family’s rise.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.069
The year Herod turned six, Antipater got his
chance.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:31.489
In 67 BC, Alexandra-Salome, the Queen of Judea,
died.

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:36.760
Unfortunately, she died without leaving a
clear line of succession, and her two sons

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.510
elected to take their claims to the battlefield.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:45.390
And so began the first in a series of wars
that are gonna wrack Judea for the rest of

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.390
this video.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.640
On one side was the Queen’s son Hyrcanus
II.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.700
On the other was Hyrcanus’s brother Aristobulus.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.470
Spoiler alert: you only need to remember Hyrcanus.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:59.390
As the war got underway, Hyrcanus came to
Herod’s dad to beg for his support.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:03.110
Antipater agreed, but not because he thought
Hyrcanus was in the right.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:07.540
In their short meeting, Antipater realized
just how weak and easy to manipulate Hyrcanus

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:08.540
was.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:14.440
And what could be better than having a weak,
manipulatable king on the throne who owes

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.580
you a favor?

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:20.459
By 63 BC, the war was moving decisively in
Hyrcanus’s favor.

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:23.980
Besieged in the Temple, Aristobulus was fast
running out of options.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.780
Faced with the imminent collapse of his claim
to the throne, Aristobulus decided to roll

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.580
everything on a crazy gamble.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:38.430
At the time, the Roman general Pompey was
riding around the eastern Mediterranean, conquering

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:39.790
everything in sight.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:45.510
So Aristobulus bunged the general a massive
bribe to invade Judea on his behalf.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.849
This would turn out to be a hilariously bad
move.

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:53.030
When word arrived, Pompey moved his army on
Jerusalem.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:58.310
There he assessed the situation, came to the
conclusion Aristobulus was going to lose,

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:04.400
pocketed Aristobulus’s money and sided with
Hyrcanus to defeat him!

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.640
Amusing as this was, it was also a major historical
turning point.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.860
In the wake of Aristobulus’s defeat, Pompey
annexed large parts of once-independent Judea.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:20.209
He then installed the victorious Hyrcanus
on the throne as a Roman client king.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:24.280
Just like that, the era of an independent
Jewish state came to a close.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.460
In its place began an era of a Jerusalem under
the heel of Rome.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.190
Not everyone was upset by this.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:35.270
For Herod’s dad, Antipater, Pompey’s intervention
was like winning the lottery.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.410
With Hyrcanus II in charge, Antipater began
happily cooperating with the new Roman authorities

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:43.639
and establishing himself as the real power
behind the throne.

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:47.380
Not that the population of Judea made this
easy.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:53.509
In 57 BC, an anti-Roman uprising forced Hyrcanus
and Antipater to call the Roman Army back

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:55.050
to save their butts.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.110
This time, Mark Antony answered the call.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:01.940
Like Pompey before him, Mark Antony subdued
Jerusalem with ease.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:06.130
In the aftermath of the campaign, he was introduced
to Antipater’s now 16-year old son.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.500
Despite being ten years older than Herod,
Mark Antony hit it off with the boy.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.930
Pretty soon, they were firm friends.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:23.069
It was a friendship that would soon make Herod
the most-powerful man in Judea.

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:24.949
The Winds of Change
There’s a peculiar curse that afflicts small

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:27.699
states on the doorsteps of superpowers.

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:31.961
Each time the winds of change ripple through
the corridors of power, the smaller state

00:05:31.961 --> 00:05:37.180
has to hunker down in case everything it holds
dear is blown away.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.370
In Rome, in 53 BC, a hurricane was brewing.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:46.540
For seven years, the Republic had been under
the sway of something called the First Triumvirate,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:51.790
a collection of three great men, all hoping
to one day become undisputed ruler.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:55.599
The first was Pompey, the old benefactor of
Antipater and Hyrcanus II.

00:05:55.599 --> 00:05:58.580
The second was a man known as Crassus.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.069
The third was Julius Caesar.

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:05.419
In May, 53 BC, Crassus died, ending the First
Triumvirate.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:10.650
With the third part of their trio gone, it
could only be so long before Caesar and Pompey

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:11.650
turned on one another.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:15.090
In January, 49 BC, they did.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:21.259
The civil war that ripped through the Roman
Republic smashed apart old certainties everywhere.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:25.650
In Judea, a presumably panicked Antipater
and Hyrcanus were forced to choose who to

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:30.389
throw their support behind: Pompey, the man
who had given them power; or Caesar, the man

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:32.360
Mark Antony now fought for?

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.430
In the end, Antipater went for Caesar.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.120
It could have been a catastrophe, the first
step along a path that ended with the victorious

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.699
Pompey having Antipater’s entire family
crucified.

00:06:42.699 --> 00:06:45.729
Instead, it became the making of them.

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:51.690
On August 9, 48 BC, Caesar annihilated Pompey’s
army at the Battle of Pharsalus.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:55.330
Broken, Pompey ran, only to be assassinated
later that year.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:07:00.330
In the aftermath, you better believe ol’
Julius didn’t forget who his friends were.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:06.789
In 47 BC, Antipater was rewarded for ditching
Pompey by being made procurator (or financial

00:07:06.789 --> 00:07:07.789
governor) of Judea.

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:12.599
Not only that, but he was given Roman citizenship,
something he was able to pass onto his son.

00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:16.870
And Antipater had big plans for his new, citizen
son.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:22.139
That same year, 47 BC, Antipater pulled some
strings and had the 26 year old Herod made

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:23.780
Governor of Galilee.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:27.849
This was the first time the ancient world
got a good look at the sort of ruler Herod

00:07:27.849 --> 00:07:28.849
would be.

00:07:28.849 --> 00:07:33.400
The results were as shocking as you might
expect.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:38.139
As Governor of Galilee, Herod’s first task
was to take care of some partisans who were

00:07:38.139 --> 00:07:42.849
hiding out in caves, trying to overthrow the
Roman-backed state and calling themselves

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:44.120
the “new Maccabees”.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.340
Everyone expected Herod to wage a conventional
war.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.939
Instead, the new governor located the cave
system the rebels were hiding in, and sent

00:07:50.939 --> 00:07:53.949
in soldiers with orders to massacre everyone.

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:58.690
It was a day of bloodshed so great that it
triggered riots in Jerusalem.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:02.150
Herod was actually hauled before the religious
authorities and threatened with death for

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.150
his actions.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.280
But Herod stood his ground.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.500
Before the highest priests of Judaism, he
asserted his right as governor to deal with

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:13.500
rebels as he saw fit, including wholesale
murder.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.470
Despite his brutality, Herod was let off free,
escaping the death penalty.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:22.530
It was the first, major scandal he was implicated
in.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.080
It wouldn’t be the last.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:34.430
The Man who would be King
If the people of Judea were hoping the days

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:40.529
of political shockwaves from Rome battering
them senseless were over, events soon proved

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:41.529
otherwise.

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:46.720
Just three years after one civil war propelled
Herod and Antipater to the top, another came

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.160
along that threatened to destroy them.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:55.829
On March 15, 44 BC, Julius Caesar was assassinated
in Rome by Brutus and Cassius.

00:08:55.829 --> 00:08:59.820
The deed done, the two traitors fled to the
east of the Republic.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:03.870
Back in the Eternal City, Mark Antony and
Caesar’s nephew Octavian declared a new

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.700
war to exterminate the assassins.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:11.390
Unfortunately for Judea, this meant once again
being caught right in the middle of things.

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:16.270
From his new powerbase in the east, Cassius
ordered all provinces to pay for his war against

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:17.920
Mark Antony and Octavian.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:25.690
That meant Judea coughing up 15,000kg of silver,
or its inhabitants being put to the sword.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:29.980
As procurator, the job of collecting that
money fell to Antipater.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:34.940
It’s possible to read Antipater’s willingness
to cooperate with Cassius as a way of protecting

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:35.940
Herod.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:40.750
But, given what we know about Antipater’s
opportunism, it’s just as easy to imagine

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:44.580
he’d flipped his loyalties once again, like
a weasel playing Pogs.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:47.380
The people of Judea certainly saw it as the
latter.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:52.510
In 43 BC, one of Antipater’s own tax collectors
assassinated him.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:56.300
When Herod got the news that his father had
been killed, he flew into a rage.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:00.700
With permission from Cassius’s forces, he
tracked down the assassin and murdered him.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:04.760
It could easily have been Herod’s last ever
act of violence.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:10.280
In 42 BC, Mark Antony and Octavian – now
formed into the Second Triumvirate alongside

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:14.300
Marcus Aemilius Lepidus – defeated Cassius
and Brutus.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:18.930
With the war over, Mark Antony went from warrior
to avenging angel, riding across the provinces

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:24.430
that had supported Cassius, leaving death
and destruction in his wake.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:28.750
This should’ve been bad news for Herod,
who was suddenly stuck with explaining why

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:31.990
Antipater had leaped into bed with Cassius.

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:36.750
But Herod had apparently picked up a trick
or two from his old man.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:40.620
He told the avenging angel “well of course
daddy had to support Cassius.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.399
He’d have destroyed Judea if he didn’t.

00:10:43.399 --> 00:10:49.320
But honestly, bro, we always wanted you to
win.”

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.080
How true this was is up for debate.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.600
But Mark Antony trusted his old friendship
with Herod enough to buy it.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:00.720
Rather than kill him, he made Herod tetrarch
of Galilee – it’s ruler.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.480
And he made Herod’s brother tetrarch of
Jerusalem.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:07.280
Suddenly, Hyrcanus II was reduced to a figurehead.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.630
Real power in Judea now lay in the hands of
Antipater’s sons.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:15.089
This turned out to be a hard sell for Judea’s
population.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:20.029
Remember, Herod’s family were seen as not
really Jewish, but fake Jews who’d got in

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:21.829
bed with the hated Romans.

00:11:21.829 --> 00:11:27.480
So when some dudes called the Parthians suddenly
attacked in 40 BC, most Jews decided to side

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:28.480
with them.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.580
Amid the fighting, Hyrcanus II was put in
chains, had his ears sliced off, and was dragged

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.620
away to captivity in Babylon.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.180
Herod’s brother, the tetrarch of Jerusalem,
committed suicide rather than face the same

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:40.180
fate.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.620
Of the three rulers of Judea, only Herod escaped.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:48.480
As the Parthians rampaged, he ran to the only
safe place he could think of: Rome.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.639
In the Eternal City, Herod was forced to beg
the Second Triumvirate for help.

00:11:52.639 --> 00:11:56.720
Mark Antony and Octavian listened and finally
agreed.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:58.000
On one condition.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.730
Herod would have to prove himself worthy by
retaking Jerusalem.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:06.190
For the next three years, Herod led a Roman
army against the Parthian puppet government

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:07.329
ruling Judea.

00:12:07.329 --> 00:12:12.029
The bitter civil war claimed many lives and
ruined Herod’s reputation further among

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:13.839
the Jewish population.

00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:14.839
But it worked.

00:12:14.839 --> 00:12:18.820
In 37 BC, Jerusalem fell and Herod rode into
the city.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.070
Aged just 36, Herod had done it.

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:25.930
He’d taken back what was his, and proved
himself worthy to his Roman clients.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:29.149
That same year, he had his highest honor yet
bestowed upon him.

00:12:29.149 --> 00:12:33.139
No longer would Herod be merely tetrarch of
Galilee, ruling alongside another tetrarch

00:12:33.139 --> 00:12:34.990
and a figurehead like Hyrcanus.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:40.360
No, from this point on, there would be only
one title befitting a warrior like Herod.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:43.870
He would be King of Judea.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:50.790
Death of a Republic
Herod’s first few years on the throne were

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:55.510
spent trying to desperately apply a veneer
of legitimacy to his rule.

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:59.560
This meant divorcing his first wife, banishing
her from court, and remarrying a woman named

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.760
Mariamne, who just happened to be a princess
from the old ruling dynasty.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:09.620
It also meant begging the now-defeated Parthians
to send Hyrcanus II back from Babylon.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.580
This might seem an odd choice.

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:15.350
Hyrcanus was the only other guy around with
a legitimate claim to Herod’s throne.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:19.230
Unfortunately for Herod, though, the captured
king was still popular in Judea.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:23.940
So Herod arranged to have Hyrcanus brought
back and treated with the utmost respect – on

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:29.640
the unspoken condition that Hyrcanus go around
being all like “Gee, this Herod sure is

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:30.640
a great king.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:33.560
We should probably do what he says.”

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.459
This done, Herod went about winning over the
populace.

00:13:36.459 --> 00:13:40.180
Over the next five years, he built massive
new city walls for Jerusalem, and a brand

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:44.920
new citadel to protect the Temple – named
Antonia, after Mark Antony, naturally.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:49.200
He even had coins minted showing himself alongside
traditional Jewish objects, just to hammer

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.770
home the point that he really was a Jewish
king.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:58.779
But, as we’ve already seen time and again
in this video, the fate of Judean rulers wasn’t

00:13:58.779 --> 00:14:02.350
decided in Judea itself, but in Rome.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:05.940
And Rome was already gearing up for yet another
civil war.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:11.670
In 33 BC, the Second Triumvirate went the
way of the First Triumvirate and unceremoniously

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:12.670
collapsed.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:17.130
Two years after that, in 31 BC, Octavian and
Mark Antony decided to fight each other to

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.130
the death.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:21.850
For Herod, this was the first time he’d
found himself caught between two Roman factions

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:24.110
without Antipater around.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:28.650
This was a problem, because Herod lacked Antipater’s
wily political instincts.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:32.920
While his father had been happy to chuck old
allies like Pompey and Caesar when he thought

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:38.279
the winds were blowing against them, Herod
was just too loyal to his friends.

00:14:38.279 --> 00:14:43.520
As the armies of Octavian and Antony gathered,
Herod threw in his lot with Antony.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.660
You can probably guess where this is going.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:52.259
On September 2, 31 BC, Octavian demolished
Antony’s forces at the Battle of Actium.

00:14:52.259 --> 00:14:56.360
Antony was forced to flee into hiding, where
he and Cleopatra committed suicide.

00:14:56.360 --> 00:15:01.990
At the same time, a summons landed on Herod’s
desk, calling him to Rhodes to explain himself

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:02.990
to Octavian.

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:07.750
If you’ve ever had to go before your boss
after making some colossal mistake, you may

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:13.480
have the faintest inkling of what Herod was
feeling on that long and lonely boat ride.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:17.490
Unlike when he had to explain Antipater’s
actions to Mark Antony, Herod had no friendship

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.800
with Octavian he could fall back on.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:25.139
Worse, as a guy who had literally named citadels
after the defeated Antony, Herod was intimately

00:15:25.139 --> 00:15:26.820
connected to Octavian’s enemy.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:31.779
Perhaps it says something of Herod’s state
of mind that his last act before leaving Judea

00:15:31.779 --> 00:15:34.320
was to execute Hyrcanus II.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:39.160
That way, if he Herod was executed in turn,
at least the throne would stay in his family.

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:43.279
But it turned out Herod really could be as
wily as Antipater.

00:15:43.279 --> 00:15:46.180
Once in Rhodes, Herod didn’t even bother
trying to lie.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:50.480
He boldly told Octavian that he had supported
Mark Antony to the death, that he’d been

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:54.740
loyal to the general for decades, and would
remain loyal even now.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:59.329
Octavian listened and, when Herod was done,
presumably gave a wry smile, before saying

00:15:59.329 --> 00:16:04.120
that Herod’s loyalty was a far more useful
asset to Rome than someone who would try and

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:05.600
worm their way out of trouble.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.470
He then reconfirmed Herod as King of Judea.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.269
It’s hard to imagine what must have been
going through Herod’s mind on the boat ride

00:16:13.269 --> 00:16:14.269
home.

00:16:14.269 --> 00:16:18.320
Against all the odds, his truth-telling gamble
had paid off.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:22.190
Octavian might not exactly trust him, but
he certainly respected him.

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:28.430
And in Octavian’s good books was exactly
where the King of Judea wanted to be.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:32.380
Some three years after the meeting in Rhodes,
Octavian finally completed his takeover of

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:33.459
the Roman Republic.

00:16:33.459 --> 00:16:38.339
He took the name history now knows him by:
Augustus.

00:16:38.339 --> 00:16:43.050
After a half century of bloodshed, Rome’s
transformation into an Empire was finally

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:44.790
complete.

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:52.319
The Good King
With the drama of the Empire’s creation

00:16:52.319 --> 00:16:58.170
out of the way, Herod was able to finally
settle into actually ruling Judea.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:04.350
Between 27 and 20 BC, he built up an image
as a sort of Pagan Jewish ruler, one he hoped

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:08.530
would appeal both to Judea’s population
and his Roman backers.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:13.210
But if neither side was ever really convinced
by Herod’s posing, they couldn’t ignore

00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:14.720
his material achievements.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:20.030
As King of Judea, Herod spent his time building
new stuff like a cocaine-addicted Bob the

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:21.030
Builder.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:25.652
Jerusalem got a new amphitheater, market,
royal palace, and a ton of new homes to replace

00:17:25.652 --> 00:17:27.570
those destroyed by an earthquake.

00:17:27.570 --> 00:17:30.250
New aqueducts brought fresh water into the
city.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:34.860
On the fringes of Judea, huge new fortresses
were built to protect the kingdom.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:39.800
Some of these, such as Masada, are still extant
and still impressive.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.830
Then there was the money spent abroad, shoring
up Judea’s image.

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:47.040
Iconic cities like Damascus and Beirut were
given Herod-sponsored makeovers.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.220
When the Olympic Games threatened to go bankrupt,
Herod stepped in and saved it.

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:55.850
This largesse was accompanied by an unmistakable
increase in Judean power.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:18:00.580
Augustus twice visited Herod in Jerusalem,
and each time he handed over new land to the

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:01.580
king.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:06.610
By 20 BC, Judea had expanded to include swathes
of modern Syria, Lebanon, and Jordan.

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:10.020
As Judea’s prestige grew, those in Rome
took notice.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:14.030
Soon Jerusalem was flooded with Roman playwrights,
historians, and philosophers, all eager to

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:16.310
see the booming city state.

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:19.600
And you better believe Herod tried to use
this power for good.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.600
He was a vocal defender of Jews living in
other states, often going out of his way to

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.570
appease their Gentile hosts.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:29.750
But of course this is still Herod we’re
talking about.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:34.130
Underneath all this glitz and glamor lurked
a dark side.

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:38.650
On the streets, ordinary Judeans were suffering
enormous tax burdens to pay for Herod’s

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:39.650
projects.

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:43.740
On top of that, there were uneasy questions
hovering over Herod’s Jewishness.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:49.210
With his fondness for pagan, Roman things,
many in Jerusalem believed their ruler was

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:51.500
trying to stealthily sabotage their culture.

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:54.000
Not that it was all bad for ordinary men and
women.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:59.050
During the horrific famine of 25 – 24 BC,
it was Herod’s Roman contacts that stopped

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:03.140
the disaster by arranging huge shipments of
grain to Judea.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:08.980
But it wasn’t until 20 BC that Herod’s
made his greatest attempt to win Jewish hearts.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:11.940
He decided to rebuild the Second Temple.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:16.340
Way back in 519 BC, the Neo-Babylonians had
destroyed the First Temple.

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:20.590
A replacement had since been built, but it
was the very definition of “modest”.

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:23.130
Under Herod, that was about to change.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:27.440
Everything you think of when you think of
Jerusalem’s Temple was a result of Herod’s

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:28.440
construction spree.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:32.300
The sweeping walls, vast towers, and great
courtyard covering the Temple Mount all came

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:33.820
into being because of Herod.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:36.340
The famous Western Wall you can still see
standing today?

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.590
That’s another Herod project.

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:41.910
Not that this grand new Temple mollified everyone.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:47.000
Herod kitted it out with too many Roman touches,
too many architectural nods to Judea’s imperial

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.020
overlords.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:49.980
Nor did he take criticism kindly.

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:54.530
When a small sect of radical Jews tore down
a Roman eagle symbol from the new Temple,

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.380
Herod had them all burned alive.

00:19:56.380 --> 00:20:01.350
Still, as Herod entered the last decade of
his life in 14 BC, the signs were all there

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:04.700
that he might go down in history as a not
so bad ruler.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:08.710
That he might even live up to his billing
of Herod the Great.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:14.290
But you and I both know that there’s one
more act to come.

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:21.270
The Massacre of the Innocents
One of the fascinating things about Herod

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:26.190
is that, despite still being a household name
2,000 years later, he often feels like he

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:29.540
was only ever a bit player in two major stories.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:33.480
For most of his life, the story Herod appeared
in was the epic novel The Rise of the Roman

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:34.480
Empire.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:38.200
But, as he entered his old age, the King of
Judea found himself cast as the villain in

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:40.210
an even more famous tale.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:43.390
That’s right, it’s time for us to talk
about Jesus.

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:52.010
There’s a long-running debate about Christ’s
exact birthdate, with anywhere from 6 BC to

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:53.010
4 BC being generally accepted.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:57.330
Whichever is true, by the time Christ was
born, Herod’s rule was in a very bad place.

00:20:57.330 --> 00:21:02.380
The trouble had really started in 10 BC, when
Herod had waged war against the Nabateans

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:06.830
and so screwed up the campaign that he’d
fallen from Augustus’s favor.

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:12.760
This sudden demotion in status seems to have
triggered a crisis of paranoia in the king.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:16.690
Convinced supporters of the dead Hyrcanus
II were plotting in the shadows, Herod began

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:19.640
a ruthless campaign to root out suspected
enemies.

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:25.320
He’d already put his wife Mariamne to death,
but now he also had her brother, mother, grandfather

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.440
and children all killed.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:31.880
Herod even had two of his own sons, Antipater
and Aristobulus, executed.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:36.310
When Augustus heard, he joked it would be
safer to be Herod’s pet pig than one of

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:37.420
his sons.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:39.540
Nor were those outside Herod’s court safe.

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:44.100
In his last years, Herod had hundreds of ordinary
Judeans thrown in dungeons and tortured.

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:46.980
He masterminded gruesome public executions
to keep order.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:50.940
Rumors started to swirl that he had defiled
the tombs of David and Solomon.

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:54.910
That he’d taken nine wives, in defiance
of Jewish customs.

00:21:54.910 --> 00:21:58.170
Although there were still triumphs – such
as the opening of Herod’s great new port

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:05.080
city of Caesarea in 9 BC – they were overshadowed
by a cloud of paranoia hanging over Judea.

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:10.140
It wasn’t helped by a horrific illness the
king contracted that ate away at his flesh,

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:12.750
thought today to have been Fournier's gangrene.

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:17.040
The disease left Herod so mad that he gave
insane orders, from the mass execution of

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:22.000
300 soldiers to a demand that all the Judean
nobles be dragged to an amphitheater on his

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.080
death and shot full of arrows.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:28.140
It was against this dark background that a
sect of Jewish scholars suddenly announced

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:31.160
the time of the Messiah was almost upon them.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.860
And so we come to Herod’s most infamous
legacy.

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:40.920
Matthew 2:16-18 tells us that Herod became
worried about the prophesized Messiah, eventually

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:45.700
ordering every child under two in Bethlehem
put to death.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:48.750
Known as the Massacre of the Innocents, the
killing is controversial among historians

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:52.210
today because it only appears in Matthew’s
gospel.

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:56.180
The other gospels don’t mention it, nor
do the accounts of Josephus or Nicolaus of

00:22:56.180 --> 00:22:57.180
Damascus.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:23:01.880
Still, from what we know of Herod’s mental
state in his last years, having a bunch of

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:05.810
random babies killed would’ve been completely
in character.

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:10.570
Thankfully, the massacre would be the last
awful thing the King of Judea ever did.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:16.790
In 4 BC Herod the Great finally passed away
in Jericho, only a handful of months after

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:18.210
a failed suicide attempt.

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:22.650
Traditionally, it’s thought he died in March,
although no-one really knows for sure.

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:26.720
After his passing, Augustus decreed Judea
would be split into three parts.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:30.430
The kingdom Herod had helped create was no
more.

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:35.970
In the end, the life of Herod was defined
less by who he was than by those who were

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:36.970
around him.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:42.210
The lives of Julius Caesar, Mark Antony, Augustus
and, of course, Jesus continue to fascinate

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:44.730
us even now, two millennia later.

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:49.580
To have been a part of all their stories is
a remarkable achievement in itself, even if

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:51.900
Herod may not have seen it that way.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:57.060
The poem Ozymandias by Percy Shelley tells
of a traveler who finds a great, broken statue

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:59.010
of some king in the middle of a desert.

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:04.110
Although the statue’s pedestal declares
“Look on my works, ye mighty, and despair!”

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:07.000
there is nothing left of this dead king’s
deeds.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:09.160
No hints of his life.

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:10.420
Just endless sand.

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:15.530
In the desert of history, it’s tempting
to think the same has come of Herod, with

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:21.830
his name still only just about visible thanks
to a single verse in a single book of the

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:22.830
Bible.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:27.950
But, as this video has hopefully shown, there
was more to the life of Herod than one horrific

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:28.950
act.

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:32.760
He may not have been a good man, or even a
particularly clever man, but Herod the Great

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:34.870
was certainly one thing.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:50.289
He was a man worth remembering.

